From: NeoDarwin
Subject: [PATCH] su: PAM policy with pam_unix in place of pam_opendirectory

su.pam (/etc/pam.d/su) authenticates through pam_opendirectory, which needs
closed frameworks (OpenDirectory, CoreFoundation); OpenPAM fails the whole
policy when a module doesn't load. Use OpenPAM's pam_unix instead, with
nullok: root has an empty password, and pam_group's check (admin or wheel,
patched in pam_modules to read /etc/group) still decides who may become
root. The session module, pam_launchd, moves su's process into a per-user
launchd, which launchd-842 doesn't run on NeoDarwin yet: pam_permit takes
its place, since a policy without a session chain falls back to "other"'s
pam_deny (docs/base/session.md, "PAM").

--- a/su/su.pam
+++ b/su/su.pam
@@ -1,7 +1,7 @@
 # su: auth account session
 auth       sufficient     pam_rootok.so 
-auth       required       pam_opendirectory.so
+auth       required       pam_unix.so nullok
 account    required       pam_group.so no_warn group=admin,wheel ruser root_only fail_safe
-account    required       pam_opendirectory.so no_check_shell
-password   required       pam_opendirectory.so
-session    required       pam_launchd.so
+account    required       pam_unix.so
+password   required       pam_unix.so
+session    required       pam_permit.so
